@PaymentApi_HostAddress = http://localhost:5103
@Keycloak_HostAddress = http://localhost:8180

### Start the AppHost first. Every service checks the token, so run the login first.
### Orders calls the payment service through the gateway, which forwards the token of the caller. These requests go straight to port 5103.

### Login of alice (password: alice) => OK, with the access token for the requests below
# @name alice
POST {{Keycloak_HostAddress}}/realms/cat-demo/protocol/openid-connect/token
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

grant_type=password&client_id=cat-demo-cli&username=alice&password=alice

### The order number is the idempotency key. The same number is charged once and always gets the same answer.
### The failures are settings of the service, not of a call. See the demo readme, chapter Payment service.

### PaymentRequest => OK, approved (an amount up to 500 euro)
POST {{PaymentApi_HostAddress}}/payment/payments HTTP/1.1
Authorization: Bearer {{alice.response.body.$.access_token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "orderNumber": "20261106-9001",
  "amount": 42.50
}

### PaymentRequest => OK, the same answer and the same reference as above, and nothing is charged again
POST {{PaymentApi_HostAddress}}/payment/payments HTTP/1.1
Authorization: Bearer {{alice.response.body.$.access_token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "orderNumber": "20261106-9001",
  "amount": 42.50
}

### PaymentRequest => OK, declined (an amount above 500 euro)
POST {{PaymentApi_HostAddress}}/payment/payments HTTP/1.1
Authorization: Bearer {{alice.response.body.$.access_token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "orderNumber": "20261106-9002",
  "amount": 500.01
}

### PaymentRequest => Bad request, no order number and no amount
POST {{PaymentApi_HostAddress}}/payment/payments HTTP/1.1
Authorization: Bearer {{alice.response.body.$.access_token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "orderNumber": "",
  "amount": 0
}
